# The tennis-racket theorem: unstable intermediate-axis tumbling — dynamic notebook
#
# A torque-free cuboid spun about its intermediate principal axis is
# unstable (Dzhanibekov effect). Half-extents [0.5, 1, 2] at mass 3 give
# principal moments [5, 4.25, 1.25]; spinning about y (4.25, the middle
# value) with a 0.01 wobble makes the body flip near t ~ 40 (mid-flip
# w_x ~ 1.54, clean spin again by t ~ 80) — yet L = I.w = [0.05, 12.75,
# 0.0125] and E = 19.1253125 never move: watch the hud E/P/L stay pinned
# while the long box tumbles end over end. The Rust example
# tumbling_body.rs (1e-4 wobble variant) and userguide Example S11 are
# this same physics — one notebook covers all three.
#
# Launch:   posim_notebook tumbling_body
#     or:   cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/tumbling_body.posim
# Press Start in the scene window (or type SCENE START here) to run
# and display the simulation.

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.01, 3, 0.01] }

# Baselines before running: the diagonal inertia [5, 4.25, 1.25], the
# perturbed spin, the conserved L = I.w, and the rotational energy.
get obj0.inertia_tensor
get obj0.angular_velocity
angmom
energy

scene create
# 0.05 per tick at ~30 ticks/s -> the t ~ 40 first flip arrives in
# about 27 wall seconds.
scene set_time_step 0.05
